1181
01:43:52,776 --> 01:44:02,048
So when we speak about language access we focus a lot on thinking that it's about translation, we actually have a community of people that are isolated.

1182
01:44:02,866 --> 01:44:08,327
because we don't have the ability to provide ASL interpretation.

1183
01:44:08,827 --> 01:44:18,409
Because I work for a municipality, it's very difficult for me to deviate from best practices and utilize, you know, friends of friends.

1184
01:44:19,410 --> 01:44:28,031
I believe that I need to give this the same gusto that I did with state government, which is utilize the Mass Commission for the Deaf and Hard of Hearing.

1185
01:44:28,812 --> 01:44:31,072
And events and things that we have,

1186
01:44:31,780 --> 01:44:38,347
We should have that already established where an ASL interpreter is available.

1187
01:44:39,248 --> 01:44:42,191
ASL interpretation is extremely different.

1188
01:44:42,291 --> 01:44:45,835
It is not AI captioning.

1189
01:44:46,312 --> 01:44:51,134
What we see on Zoom and live transcription is not closed captioning.

1190
01:44:51,635 --> 01:45:00,659
Visual language users don't speak in the same sentences and the same English language structure that we do.

1191
01:45:01,099 --> 01:45:03,501
And that's why ASL is important.

1192
01:45:04,341 --> 01:45:06,562
That's why having that is important.

1193
01:45:06,922 --> 01:45:10,024
That's why sometimes the readability

1194
01:45:10,655 --> 01:45:18,762
is not accessible because the information cannot be processed and retained because the language structure is just not like that.

1195
01:45:18,862 --> 01:45:33,155
So we have to think about how do we move on to the video monologues where maybe our meetings like this are in real life being shown to people who may have something to add.

1196
01:45:34,112 --> 01:45:38,335
So the ADA aspects are an immediate need.

1197
01:45:38,935 --> 01:45:41,637
